Description: Computes and analyses the strong binary relationships of a
    variability model (a feature model, or the configuration space of a
    system such as the Linux kernel): which features require others,
    which exclude each other and of which pairs at least one must be
    selected, in every valid configuration. Models are read in the
    Universal Variability Language ('UVL') or as Boolean formulas in
    'DIMACS' format, such as those that 'KMax', 'KConfigReader',
    'KconfigSampler' or 'TraVarT' produce from other notations. A
    satisfiability solver ('Glucose') finds the relationships; the
    resulting graphs are written as 'Pajek' files and analysed with more
    than one hundred functions that return tibbles: atomic sets,
    hierarchy, conflicts, configuration and team planning, combinatorial
    testing, network patterns and the evolution of a model through its
    versions. Rules that involve three or more features at once are not
    represented in the graphs. A companion data set of graphs for 5,709
    models is available at <doi:10.5281/zenodo.17277788>.
